  "query_preview": "..\n(Tuesday night through next Monday)\nIssued at 1150 PM CST Sun Nov 15 2026\n\nAfter chilly temperatures on Monday, Tuesday will be warmer for\nour area as winds become southeast. Highs Tuesday will be in the \nupper 50s to lower 60s, with considerable cloud cover. Moist \nsoutherly low-level flow will continue Wednesday into Friday. \nThis will result in persistent cloud cover over most of our area, \nalthough our eastern counties near the edge of the low-level moist\naxis should have some breaks in the cloud cover. Despite the \ncloud cover, the southerly low-level flow will facilitate a \nwarming trend. Highs are expected to be in the mid to upper 60s \nWednesday, and upper 60s to lower 70s Thursday and Friday. Cloud \ncover and south-southeast winds at night will keep overnight lows \nmilder as well, in the lower to mid 50s Wednesday night and mostly\nin the mid 50s Thursday and Friday nights.\n\nOur area will be under southwest flow aloft in the mid-to-late week \ntime frame with an upper trough lifting from the central/northern \nRockies into the central/northern Plains Wednesday and Wednesday \nnight, and an upstream trough moving from the Intermountain West and \nGreat Basin into the Rockies on Friday. This system is progged to \nmove east into the central and northern Plains by Saturday morning, \nwith associated cold front moving south across our area Saturday \nafternoon into Saturday night. While the medium range models have \nincreased differences by Day 7 and beyond, there are indications \nthat our area will have a potential for showers and thunderstorms \nwith the arrival of the cold front Saturday, and with the possible\ndevelopment of an overrunning setup Sunday. Model blends have low\nrain chances (15-30 percent) across at least some of our area \nSaturday and Sunday.",
